  "dom_id": "step-q121919",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "The velocity vector $v=\u27e8u,v\u27e9$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=\u27e8\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3t+1),1-{t}^{2}\u27e9,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=2.$",
  "worked_solution": "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3t+1)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{3}{3t+1},-2t\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2) & =\u27e8\\frac{3}{3(2)+1},-2(2)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{3}{7},-4\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$",
